/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: BC-110 Basic Incident Response & Reporting */
window.COURSE_DATA = window.COURSE_DATA || {};
window.COURSE_DATA["BC-110"] = {
ov:"When prevention fails — and it will — the speed and discipline of the response determine the damage. This course builds the incident response fundamentals: the NIST lifecycle, triage, containment, eradication and recovery, evidence handling, documentation, and reporting. Each module ends in a required knowledge check; the 40-item capstone draws two items per module plus applied scenarios, so treat the procedures as testable.",
mods:[
{t:"The Incident Response Lifecycle",d:"NIST SP 800-61's four phases.",
obj:["Name the phases of the NIST incident response lifecycle","Explain how the phases cycle and feed back","Explain why preparation is the foundation"],
read:["NIST SP 800-61 defines the incident response lifecycle in four phases: Preparation (building the capability before anything happens), Detection and Analysis (identifying and understanding incidents), Containment, Eradication, and Recovery (limiting damage, removing the threat, and restoring operations), and Post-Incident Activity (learning and improving). The phases are cyclical — lessons from post-incident activity feed back into preparation — and the boundaries blur in practice, but the framework gives responders a shared structure for what is otherwise a chaotic, high-pressure situation.",
"Preparation is the foundation because everything else depends on it: the tools, plans, training, and access assembled before an incident determine how well the response goes when the clock is running. An organization that builds its IR capability during an incident is already losing. The lifecycle framing also emphasizes that response is not just the dramatic containment phase — detection quality determines how early you engage, and post-incident learning determines whether the next incident goes better. This is the resilience mindset from BC-101 made operational: assume breach, and be ready to absorb, respond, and recover."],
ex:"An organization detects ransomware. Because it prepared — an IR plan, an on-call team, isolated backups, and practiced procedures — it moves smoothly through detection and analysis, containment, eradication, and recovery, then holds a lessons-learned review that improves the plan. An unprepared organization facing the same event improvises under pressure, makes costly mistakes, and takes far longer to recover. The lifecycle, especially preparation, made the difference.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2","The incident response lifecycle and its phases."],["SANS Incident Handler's Handbook","A practical six-step handling process mapping to the NIST phases."]],
kc:[
{q:"The four phases of the NIST incident response lifecycle are:",o:["Plan, build, ship, monitor","Preparation; Detection & Analysis; Containment, Eradication & Recovery; Post-Incident Activity","Scan, patch, report, close","Categorize, select, implement, assess"],a:1,x:"NIST SP 800-61 defines these four cyclical phases, with lessons feeding back into preparation."},
{q:"Preparation is the foundation of incident response because:",o:["It is the last phase","The tools, plans, training, and access assembled beforehand determine how well the response goes under pressure","It replaces detection","It is optional"],a:1,x:"An organization building its capability during an incident is already losing; readiness before the event is decisive."},
{q:"The incident response lifecycle is described as cyclical because:",o:["It never ends","Post-incident lessons feed back into preparation, improving future responses","Incidents repeat exactly","It has no phases"],a:1,x:"Learning closes the loop; each incident's lessons strengthen the preparation for the next — the resilience mindset operationalized."}]},
{t:"Preparation: Building IR Capability",d:"Plans, teams, tools, and access before the incident.",
obj:["Identify the components of IR preparation","Explain the value of an IR plan and trained team","Explain pre-staged tools and access"],
read:["Preparation assembles everything needed to respond effectively: a documented incident response plan (roles, procedures, decision authorities, communication paths), a trained and exercised response team (or retainer with an external firm), the tools to detect and investigate (logging, EDR, forensic capability), pre-established access and authority (responders can isolate a host without a week of approvals), and relationships (legal, PR, law enforcement, leadership) established before they are urgently needed. Each element removes friction from the moment when speed matters most.",
"The plan and the team are the core. A plan that sits unexercised is little better than none — the contingency-plan lesson from earlier courses — so IR plans are drilled through tabletop exercises and simulations, which reveal gaps (who has authority to disconnect the internet? who calls the lawyers?) while there is time to fix them. Pre-staged access matters because incidents move fast: if isolating a compromised host requires emergency change approvals that take hours, the attacker has hours to spread. Preparation is unglamorous investment that pays off entirely in the compressed, high-stakes minutes of an actual incident."],
ex:"During a tabletop exercise, a team discovers that no one has documented authority to disconnect a business-critical system from the network, and that the after-hours contact for legal is out of date. Both are fixed before any real incident. Months later, when a real intrusion requires exactly that authority at 2 a.m., the responder acts immediately — the exercise surfaced the gap while there was time to close it.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2, Preparation","Building the incident response capability."],["CISA, Incident Response Plan guidance","Plan components and tabletop exercises."]],
kc:[
{q:"Pre-established access and authority for responders matters because:",o:["It looks professional","Incidents move fast; if isolating a host requires hours of approvals, the attacker has hours to spread","It reduces documentation","Authority is never needed"],a:1,x:"Delays from emergency approvals give the attacker time; pre-staged authority lets responders act at incident speed."},
{q:"An incident response plan that is never exercised is:",o:["Sufficient","Little better than none — tabletop exercises reveal gaps while there is time to fix them","The best kind","Required to stay unused"],a:1,x:"Like a contingency plan, an unexercised IR plan hides gaps that only surface — expensively — during a real incident."},
{q:"A tabletop exercise primarily provides:",o:["A real incident","A safe way to reveal gaps in the plan (authorities, contacts, procedures) before a real incident","Malware analysis","A penetration test"],a:1,x:"Exercises surface missing authorities, stale contacts, and procedural gaps while there is time to remediate them."}]},
{t:"Events vs. Incidents: Triage",d:"Separating noise from real incidents.",
obj:["Distinguish an event from an incident","Explain the triage process","Explain the cost of over- and under-triaging"],
read:["Not everything is an incident. An event is any observable occurrence (a login, a connection, an alert); an incident is an event, or series of events, that actually threatens security — a confirmed intrusion, malware execution, data exposure. Triage is the process of examining events and alerts to determine which are real incidents warranting response and which are benign or false positives. Given the flood of alerts a modern environment generates (BC-102's alert-fatigue lesson), effective triage is what keeps responders focused on real threats rather than drowning in noise.",
"Triage balances two failure modes. Over-triaging (treating every alert as an incident) exhausts the team and causes real incidents to be lost in the churn. Under-triaging (dismissing alerts too readily) misses real intrusions — the classic breach where the alert fired but was closed as noise. Good triage uses context (what is the asset, does the pattern match known-bad, does it deviate from baseline) and a consistent process to make the real-or-not judgment quickly and defensibly, escalating genuine incidents into the response process and documenting the reasoning for dismissals. This is the front door of incident response, and its quality determines everything downstream."],
ex:"An analyst triages an alert: a workstation connecting to an unusual external IP. Context shows the IP is newly-registered, the connection beacons regularly, and the host's user is on vacation — this is a real incident, escalated immediately. A second alert, an admin logging in from a new laptop they just received, is verified benign and closed with documented reasoning. Consistent triage separated the real intrusion from the false positive without either drowning the team or missing the threat.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2, Detection and Analysis","Distinguishing incidents and prioritizing them."],["SANS, alert triage practices","Consistent triage to counter alert fatigue."]],
kc:[
{q:"The difference between an event and an incident is that an incident:",o:["Is any observable occurrence","Actually threatens security — a confirmed intrusion, malware, or data exposure","Is always a false positive","Involves no events"],a:1,x:"Events are observable occurrences; incidents are the subset that genuinely threaten security and warrant response."},
{q:"Under-triaging (dismissing alerts too readily) risks:",o:["Nothing","Missing real intrusions — the classic breach where the alert fired but was closed as noise","Too much work","Better security"],a:1,x:"Dismissing genuine alerts as noise is exactly how famous breaches were missed despite the detection firing."},
{q:"Effective triage uses ___ to judge whether an alert is a real incident:",o:["Random guessing","Context — asset criticality, known-bad patterns, and baseline deviation — applied through a consistent process","The alert count only","The time of day"],a:1,x:"Context-driven, consistent triage separates real incidents from noise quickly and defensibly."}]},
{t:"Detection and Analysis",d:"Identifying and understanding what is happening.",
obj:["Describe detection sources and their value","Explain analyzing an incident's scope and nature","Explain forming and testing hypotheses during analysis"],
read:["Detection and analysis is where responders identify that an incident is occurring and build understanding of what it is. Detection sources span the telemetry from earlier courses — SIEM alerts, EDR detections, IDS/IPS, log anomalies, user reports (the human sensor network), and threat-intelligence matches. The first job is confirmation (is this real?), the second is scoping (what is affected, how far has it spread, what did the attacker do?). Analysis turns a single alert into an understanding of the incident's nature, extent, and impact.",
"Analysis is investigative and hypothesis-driven: from an initial indicator, the responder asks what would be true if this were a particular kind of attack, then examines evidence to confirm or refute it, pivoting across data sources (the Diamond Model logic from BC-108) — from a suspicious process to its network connections to other affected hosts. The goal is an accurate picture of scope before acting, because containment decisions depend on knowing the extent: isolating one host when the attacker is on ten leaves nine, and a rushed response based on incomplete analysis can tip off the attacker or destroy evidence. Analysis and containment interplay — enough analysis to act wisely, without paralysis while the attacker operates."],
ex:"An EDR alert on one host leads the responder to examine its process lineage (a document spawned PowerShell that dumped credentials), then pivot to network logs (the host connected to two others using those credentials), revealing the incident's true scope: three compromised hosts, not one. Containing all three at once — informed by the analysis — prevents the attacker from simply moving to the hosts a single-host response would have missed.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2, Detection and Analysis","Confirming, scoping, and understanding incidents."],["SANS, incident analysis and scoping","Hypothesis-driven investigation and pivoting."]],
kc:[
{q:"The two primary goals of incident analysis are:",o:["Blame and punishment","Confirmation (is it real?) and scoping (what is affected and how far has it spread?)","Encryption and backup","Reporting and closing"],a:1,x:"Analysis confirms the incident and determines its extent, because containment decisions depend on knowing the true scope."},
{q:"Acting on containment before adequate analysis risks:",o:["Nothing","Missing part of the attacker's footprint — isolating one host when they are on ten leaves nine","Faster recovery","Better evidence"],a:1,x:"Incomplete scoping leads to partial containment; the attacker simply continues from the hosts the rushed response missed."},
{q:"Incident analysis is hypothesis-driven, meaning the responder:",o:["Guesses the conclusion","Forms a hypothesis about the attack and pivots across data sources to confirm or refute it","Ignores evidence","Acts before investigating"],a:1,x:"From an initial indicator, the responder tests hypotheses by pivoting across evidence — the Diamond-Model logic applied to IR."}]},
{t:"Log Analysis for Incident Response",d:"Reconstructing what happened from the evidence.",
obj:["Identify key log sources for incident reconstruction","Explain correlating logs across sources and time","Explain the importance of time synchronization"],
read:["Logs are the responder's primary evidence, and reconstructing an incident means correlating them across sources and time. The valuable sources are exactly those the earlier courses built: endpoint logs and EDR (process execution, BC-103), authentication and directory logs (BC-104), network flow, DNS, and proxy logs (BC-102), and application logs. No single source tells the whole story — endpoint logs show what ran, network logs show where it connected, authentication logs show whose credentials were used — so the responder correlates them into a coherent timeline of the attacker's actions.",
"Time synchronization is non-negotiable for this correlation. If logs from different systems have skewed clocks, the responder cannot reliably sequence events across them, and a timeline built from mis-synchronized logs produces false conclusions about cause and order. This is why NTP discipline (established in BC-102/103) matters so much for IR. The reconstruction skill — pulling the relevant window from each source, aligning them on a synchronized timeline, and reading the attacker's path from initial access through actions on objectives — is the analytical heart of investigation, and it depends entirely on the logging that preparation put in place and central collection protected from tampering."],
ex:"Reconstructing an intrusion, the responder pulls authentication logs (showing a credential used at 02:14), endpoint logs (a malicious process launched at 02:15), and network logs (exfiltration to an external IP at 02:22), aligns them on synchronized time, and reads the attacker's path clearly. Had the systems' clocks been skewed by minutes, the sequence would have been ambiguous and the timeline unreliable — synchronized logging made the reconstruction possible.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-92","Log management and correlation for investigation."],["SANS, log analysis for incident response","Correlating multi-source logs into timelines."]],
kc:[
{q:"Reconstructing an incident requires correlating logs because:",o:["One source has everything","No single source tells the whole story — endpoint, network, and authentication logs each reveal different pieces","Logs are unreliable","Correlation is optional"],a:1,x:"Endpoint logs show what ran, network logs where it connected, auth logs whose credentials — only correlation reveals the full path."},
{q:"Time synchronization (NTP) across systems is critical for IR because:",o:["It saves disk space","Skewed clocks make cross-source event sequencing unreliable, producing false timelines","It encrypts logs","It is legally required only"],a:1,x:"Correlating events across systems demands aligned clocks; skew corrupts the order and causation the timeline depends on."},
{q:"The most valuable log sources for incident reconstruction include:",o:["Only the firewall","Endpoint/EDR, authentication/directory, and network (flow/DNS/proxy) logs together","Only email","None"],a:1,x:"These complementary sources, correlated on a synchronized timeline, reconstruct the attacker's path end to end."}]},
{t:"Classification and Severity",d:"Prioritizing incidents by impact and urgency.",
obj:["Explain incident classification and severity rating","Describe factors driving severity","Explain how severity drives response prioritization"],
read:["Incidents are classified (by type — malware, data breach, denial of service, insider) and rated by severity so that response is prioritized appropriately. Severity reflects impact (what is affected, how critical, how much data, what mission consequence) and urgency (is it spreading, is data actively leaving). A ransomware outbreak spreading across critical systems is high severity demanding immediate all-hands response; a single blocked malware sample on an isolated test machine is low. Consistent severity rating ensures the most damaging incidents get resources first, echoing the risk-rating discipline from assessment.",
"Severity is not static — it is reassessed as analysis reveals scope. An incident that initially appears to be one compromised host (moderate) may, on analysis, prove to be a domain-wide compromise (critical), and the response scales accordingly. Clear severity definitions, agreed in preparation, prevent both the panic of treating everything as critical and the complacency of under-rating a real threat. Severity drives concrete decisions: who is engaged, how fast, whether leadership and external parties are notified, and what containment aggressiveness is justified — connecting classification to the response actions of the following modules."],
ex:"An incident starts rated moderate (one host with malware). Analysis reveals the attacker used harvested domain-admin credentials and reached the domain controller — the severity is immediately escalated to critical, triggering executive notification, an all-hands response, and aggressive containment. The dynamic re-rating, driven by analysis, matched the response to the true threat rather than the initial appearance.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2, incident prioritization","Rating incidents by functional and information impact and recoverability."],["FIRST / sector incident severity frameworks","Consistent severity definitions."]],
kc:[
{q:"Incident severity primarily reflects:",o:["The attacker's name","Impact (what is affected and the mission consequence) and urgency (spread, active data loss)","The time of day","The tool used"],a:1,x:"Severity combines impact and urgency to prioritize response toward the most damaging incidents first."},
{q:"Incident severity should be:",o:["Fixed at the initial rating","Reassessed as analysis reveals scope — escalating (or de-escalating) to match the true threat","Ignored","Set by the vendor"],a:1,x:"Scope often changes with analysis; dynamic re-rating keeps the response proportionate to the actual threat."},
{q:"Severity drives which concrete decisions?",o:["None","Who is engaged, how fast, whether leadership and external parties are notified, and containment aggressiveness","Only the report format","The password policy"],a:1,x:"Severity determines resourcing, notification, and response intensity — connecting classification to action."}]},
{t:"Containment Strategies",d:"Limiting damage without destroying evidence.",
obj:["Explain the goals and trade-offs of containment","Distinguish short-term and long-term containment","Explain balancing containment against evidence preservation"],
read:["Containment limits the incident's spread and damage while the team works toward eradication. It balances competing goals: stopping the attacker quickly, preserving evidence for investigation, avoiding tipping off a sophisticated adversary, and maintaining business operations where possible. Short-term containment acts fast to stop immediate damage (isolating a host, blocking an IP, disabling an account); long-term containment applies more durable measures (rebuilding systems, applying patches) while planning full eradication. The strategy depends on the incident's nature and severity.",
"The key trade-off is speed versus evidence and stealth. Immediately powering off a compromised host stops the attacker but destroys volatile memory evidence and alerts them; isolating it on the network preserves the running state for forensic capture while cutting the attacker's access. For a sophisticated intrusion, responders may deliberately observe briefly to understand scope before containing everywhere at once (avoiding a partial containment that tips the attacker into destructive action or evasion). These are judgment calls made against the incident's severity and the organization's risk tolerance — there is no single right answer, only reasoned trade-offs the response plan should frame in advance."],
ex:"Facing a compromised host, the responder isolates it from the network (cutting the attacker's access and stopping spread) rather than powering it off, preserving the memory and disk state for forensic capture. For a suspected wider intrusion, they hold the isolation until analysis confirms all affected hosts, then contain them simultaneously — preventing the attacker from fleeing to hosts a piecemeal response would miss. Each choice balanced speed, evidence, and completeness.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2, Containment","Containment strategy and its trade-offs."],["SANS, containment decision-making","Balancing speed, evidence, and stealth."]],
kc:[
{q:"Containment must balance stopping the attacker against:",o:["Nothing else","Preserving evidence, avoiding tipping off the adversary, and maintaining business operations","Buying new hardware","Writing the report"],a:1,x:"Containment weighs speed against evidence preservation, stealth, and operational continuity — a set of reasoned trade-offs."},
{q:"Isolating a compromised host on the network, rather than powering it off, primarily:",o:["Is slower with no benefit","Cuts the attacker's access while preserving volatile memory and disk state for forensics","Destroys evidence","Alerts all users"],a:1,x:"Isolation stops the attacker but keeps the running system intact for forensic capture, unlike a power-off that loses memory."},
{q:"For a suspected wide intrusion, containing hosts one at a time can:",o:["Improve evidence","Tip off the attacker, who flees to hosts a piecemeal response has not yet contained","Speed up recovery","Preserve stealth"],a:1,x:"Partial containment can alert a sophisticated adversary; simultaneous containment after scoping prevents them escaping the response."}]},
{t:"Host Isolation and Network Containment",d:"The mechanics of cutting off the threat.",
obj:["Describe technical methods of host and network isolation","Explain account and credential containment","Explain containing at the identity and network layers"],
read:["Containment is executed through concrete technical methods drawn from the earlier courses. Host isolation removes a compromised system from the network while keeping it powered for forensics — via EDR network-isolation features (which cut all traffic except to the response console), switch-port disabling, or firewall rules. Network containment blocks the attacker's infrastructure — egress filtering of C2 domains and IPs (BC-102), segmentation to prevent lateral movement, and null-routing malicious destinations. These stop the attacker's communication and movement without destroying the evidence on the isolated hosts.",
"Identity-layer containment is often the most important and most overlooked: because credential theft drives lateral movement (BC-104), containing an incident frequently requires disabling compromised accounts, forcing password resets, and — in a domain compromise — resetting the KRBTGT account (twice) to invalidate forged tickets. Simply isolating hosts while leaving stolen credentials valid lets the attacker return through other systems. Effective containment therefore acts at multiple layers simultaneously — host, network, and identity — matching the layered nature of the intrusion. The mechanics matter because a containment that misses a layer (isolates hosts but not accounts) leaves a door open."],
ex:"Responding to a domain compromise, the team isolates the known-compromised hosts via EDR, blocks the C2 infrastructure at the egress firewall, and — critically — disables the compromised accounts and resets KRBTGT to invalidate the attacker's forged golden tickets. Isolating hosts alone would have left the attacker able to return using stolen credentials; the identity-layer containment closed the door the host isolation could not.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2 and SP 800-53 IR controls","Containment execution."],["Microsoft, recovering from domain compromise (KRBTGT reset)","Identity-layer containment in directory compromises."]],
kc:[
{q:"EDR network isolation of a compromised host:",o:["Powers it off","Cuts its network traffic except to the response console, preserving the host for forensics","Deletes its disk","Encrypts it"],a:1,x:"Network isolation cuts the attacker's access while keeping the running system intact for investigation."},
{q:"Why is identity-layer containment (disabling accounts, resetting credentials) often essential?",o:["It is not needed","Because credential theft drives lateral movement; isolating hosts while leaving stolen credentials valid lets the attacker return","It slows recovery","Passwords are irrelevant"],a:1,x:"Attackers move via stolen credentials; without disabling accounts and resetting keys, host isolation alone leaves an open door."},
{q:"Effective containment acts at:",o:["The host layer only","Multiple layers simultaneously — host, network, and identity — matching the intrusion's layered nature","Only the firewall","Only the identity layer"],a:1,x:"An intrusion spans hosts, network, and identity; containment that misses a layer leaves the attacker a path back in."}]},
{t:"Eradication and Recovery",d:"Removing the threat and restoring safely.",
obj:["Explain eradication and its verification","Describe safe recovery from clean state","Explain monitoring for reinfection"],
read:["Eradication removes the threat from the environment — deleting malware, closing the exploited vulnerability, removing attacker persistence (accounts, services, scheduled tasks, implants), and rebuilding compromised systems. The critical discipline is thoroughness: attackers commonly establish multiple persistence mechanisms, so eradicating the obvious malware while missing a backdoor account or a secondary implant lets them return. For significant compromises, rebuilding from known-good images rather than cleaning in place is often the only trustworthy eradication, because you cannot be certain a compromised system is fully clean.",
"Recovery restores systems to normal operation — from clean backups or rebuilt images — and validates that they are functioning and secure before returning them to production. The key principles: recover to a known-good state (not a backup that may already contain the attacker's foothold, which is why backup integrity and the RTO/RPO planning of BC-101 matter), verify systems are clean and hardened before reconnecting, and monitor closely for reinfection, because attackers frequently attempt to return through persistence the eradication missed. Recovery is not complete when systems are back up; it is complete when they are back up, verified clean, and confirmed stable under heightened monitoring."],
ex:"After eradicating ransomware, the team rebuilds affected servers from known-good images (not the potentially-compromised backups from just before the attack), resets all credentials, and monitors intensively for two weeks. On day three, monitoring catches an attempt to use a backdoor account the initial eradication missed — the heightened watch caught the attacker's return, and the account is disabled. Recovery to a clean state plus vigilant monitoring completed the eradication.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2, Eradication and Recovery","Removing threats and restoring safely."],["CISA ransomware recovery guidance","Rebuilding from clean state and monitoring for reinfection."]],
kc:[
{q:"Eradicating only the obvious malware while missing a backdoor account:",o:["Fully resolves the incident","Lets the attacker return — attackers commonly establish multiple persistence mechanisms","Improves recovery","Is best practice"],a:1,x:"Thorough eradication must remove all persistence; missing one mechanism leaves the attacker a path back in."},
{q:"For significant compromises, the most trustworthy eradication is often:",o:["Cleaning malware in place","Rebuilding from known-good images, since you cannot be certain a compromised system is fully clean","Ignoring the malware","Restoring the most recent backup blindly"],a:1,x:"In-place cleaning cannot guarantee completeness; rebuilding from clean images provides trustworthy eradication."},
{q:"Recovery is complete when systems are:",o:["Simply back online","Back up, verified clean and hardened, and confirmed stable under heightened monitoring for reinfection","Restored from any backup","Rebooted"],a:1,x:"Attackers try to return; recovery includes verification and intensive monitoring, not just bringing systems back up."}]},
{t:"Evidence Handling and Chain of Custody",d:"Preserving evidence for investigation and legal use.",
obj:["Explain proper evidence collection and preservation","Describe chain of custody and its importance","Explain evidence integrity through hashing"],
read:["Incident evidence may be needed for internal investigation, legal action, or law enforcement, so it must be collected and preserved properly from the start. The principles: collect in order of volatility (capture memory and running state before powering off, since volatile evidence disappears — connecting to the containment choice of isolating rather than powering off), work on copies while preserving originals, and hash evidence (a cryptographic checksum) at collection so any later alteration is detectable. These practices ensure the evidence is trustworthy and, where needed, admissible.",
"Chain of custody is the documented record of who handled the evidence, when, and what they did with it — an unbroken trail from collection through analysis to storage. A broken chain (evidence unaccounted for, or handled by unknown people at unknown times) undermines its credibility and admissibility, because it can no longer be shown to be unaltered and authentic. For most internal incidents, rigorous chain of custody may seem excessive, but you rarely know at the start whether an incident will become a legal matter — so treating evidence properly from the beginning, when it is cheap, avoids destroying its value when it turns out to matter. This mirrors the dated-scoped-attributable evidence discipline from the assessment and controls courses, applied under investigative stakes."],
ex:"Responding to a suspected insider data theft, the team images the disk, hashes the image at collection, works only on copies, and logs every access in a chain-of-custody form. When the incident becomes an HR and legal matter, the evidence holds up — the hash proves it is unaltered and the custody log proves who handled it. Had they investigated on the original without hashing or logging, the evidence's value would have evaporated exactly when it mattered.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-86","Guide to integrating forensic techniques into incident response."],["SANS, evidence handling and chain of custody","Collection order, integrity, and custody documentation."]],
kc:[
{q:"Evidence should be collected in order of:",o:["Alphabetical order","Volatility — capturing memory and running state before powering off, since volatile evidence disappears","File size","Random order"],a:1,x:"Volatile evidence (memory, running state) vanishes on power-off; it must be captured first — informing the isolate-not-poweroff choice."},
{q:"Hashing evidence at collection provides:",o:["Encryption","Integrity — a checksum that makes any later alteration detectable","Faster analysis","Compression"],a:1,x:"A cryptographic hash captured at collection proves the evidence is unaltered, supporting its trustworthiness and admissibility."},
{q:"A broken chain of custody:",o:["Improves the evidence","Undermines the evidence's credibility and admissibility, since it can no longer be shown unaltered and authentic","Speeds up the case","Is normal"],a:1,x:"An unbroken documented trail is what proves evidence authenticity; gaps destroy its legal and investigative value."}]},
{t:"Documentation and Timelines",d:"Recording facts clearly as the incident unfolds.",
obj:["Explain the purpose and discipline of incident documentation","Describe building an incident timeline","Explain writing factual, attributable records"],
read:["Thorough documentation is essential during and after an incident — for coordinating the response, for the post-incident review, for reporting, and for any legal proceeding. Responders record what they observe and do, with timestamps: what was found, what actions were taken, what decisions were made and why. This is done contemporaneously, because memory is unreliable under stress and the details that seem obvious in the moment are forgotten by the review. Good incident documentation is factual and precise — what happened, not speculation or blame — and attributable, so each observation and action is tied to who made it and when.",
"The incident timeline is the central documentation artifact: a chronological reconstruction of the attacker's actions (from the log correlation of module five) and the responder's actions, aligned on synchronized time. It answers the questions everyone will ask — when did the attacker get in, what did they do, when were they detected, when contained, when eradicated — and it is the backbone of both the incident report and the lessons-learned review. Writing it well means distinguishing established facts (evidenced) from assessments (inferred, with confidence), avoiding the overconfident narrative that later proves wrong. The documentation discipline mirrors the faithful-reporting ethic from the assessment course: record the truth, precisely, as you find it."],
ex:"Throughout an incident, a responder logs each action with a timestamp — 03:10 isolated host, 03:25 disabled compromised account, 03:40 confirmed exfiltration in proxy logs. Afterward, these entries plus the attacker-action reconstruction form a clear timeline for the report and review. A team that reconstructed the timeline from memory days later would have produced a vaguer, less defensible account — contemporaneous documentation preserved the facts.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2, documentation","Recording incident details and actions."],["SANS, incident documentation and timeline building","Contemporaneous, factual, attributable records."]],
kc:[
{q:"Incident documentation should be done:",o:["From memory after it ends","Contemporaneously — recording observations and actions with timestamps as they happen","Only if legal action follows","By the vendor"],a:1,x:"Memory is unreliable under stress; contemporaneous, timestamped records preserve the facts the review and report need."},
{q:"The incident timeline is:",o:["An optional extra","The central artifact — a chronological reconstruction of attacker and responder actions on synchronized time","Just the attacker's actions","Written by the attacker"],a:1,x:"The timeline answers when the attacker got in, acted, was detected, and contained — the backbone of the report and review."},
{q:"Good incident documentation distinguishes:",o:["Nothing","Established facts (evidenced) from assessments (inferred, with stated confidence), avoiding overconfident narrative","Only opinions","Blame from praise"],a:1,x:"Separating fact from inference, as in the assessment course's faithful-reporting ethic, keeps the record accurate and defensible."}]},
{t:"Escalation, Communication, and Reporting",d:"Notifying the right people at the right time.",
obj:["Explain internal escalation and communication during incidents","Describe external and regulatory reporting obligations","Explain communication discipline and stakeholder management"],
read:["Incidents require communication on two fronts. Internally, escalation follows the plan's communication matrix: the response team, management, and — for serious incidents — executives and legal are notified per defined thresholds, so decisions requiring their authority are made promptly and the organization speaks with one voice. Communication discipline matters — accurate, timely updates to stakeholders without speculation, and control over who communicates externally, because premature or inconsistent statements during an incident cause real harm. The plan defines who is informed at each severity and who is authorized to speak publicly.",
"Externally, many incidents trigger reporting obligations. Regulatory regimes impose breach-notification requirements with specific timelines (some measured in hours or days), law enforcement may be engaged, affected individuals and partners may need notification, and federal systems have their own reporting chains (US-CERT/CISA, agency requirements). These obligations are legal, not optional, and missing a notification deadline compounds the incident with a compliance failure. Because the timelines are tight and the requirements complex, the reporting obligations and contacts are mapped in preparation — you do not want to be researching breach-notification law while the clock runs. This connects to the regulatory dimension detailed further in the advanced incident-command course."],
ex:"A confirmed breach of personal data triggers the plan's escalation (executives and legal notified within the hour) and its external obligations (a regulatory notification due within 72 hours, affected-individual notice, and a CISA report). Because these obligations and contacts were mapped during preparation, the team meets every deadline. An unprepared organization, researching its obligations mid-incident, would risk missing a legally-mandated notification — turning one failure into two.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2, coordination and information sharing","Internal and external incident communication."],["CISA incident reporting requirements and breach-notification laws","External reporting obligations and timelines."]],
kc:[
{q:"Internal incident communication should be:",o:["Unrestricted speculation","Accurate, timely updates per the plan's matrix, with controlled external messaging and one voice","Whatever each person decides","Withheld from management"],a:1,x:"Disciplined, accurate communication and controlled external statements prevent the harm of premature or inconsistent messaging."},
{q:"Regulatory breach-notification requirements are:",o:["Optional suggestions","Legal obligations with specific timelines; missing a deadline adds a compliance failure to the incident","Only for large companies","Handled after full recovery"],a:1,x:"Notification is legally mandated on tight timelines; missing it compounds the incident with a regulatory violation."},
{q:"Why are external reporting obligations mapped during preparation?",o:["To fill the plan with pages","Because the timelines are tight and requirements complex — you cannot research breach law while the clock runs","They are not mapped","Reporting is never required"],a:1,x:"Tight, complex notification requirements must be understood in advance so deadlines are met during the compressed incident."}]},
{t:"IR Roles and Coordination",d:"Who does what when the alarm sounds.",
obj:["Identify the key incident response roles","Explain coordination between technical and non-technical roles","Explain the incident commander concept"],
read:["Effective response is coordinated, not chaotic, and that requires defined roles. The core technical roles include the incident handlers/analysts who investigate and contain, and specialists (forensics, malware analysis, threat intelligence) who go deep as needed. Around them, non-technical roles are essential: an incident commander or lead who coordinates the overall response and makes priority decisions, communications/PR for external messaging, legal for obligations and privilege, HR for insider cases, and executive sponsors for decisions above the team's authority. When these roles are pre-assigned (in preparation), the response flows; when they are improvised, it stalls at every decision point.",
"The incident commander concept — borrowed from emergency management — gives one person clear authority to coordinate the response, so that the team is not paralyzed by unclear decision-making during a fast-moving event. The commander does not do everything but ensures every function is covered, priorities are set, and communication flows, freeing the technical responders to focus on the investigation. Coordination between technical and non-technical roles is where many responses falter — the technical team contains the threat but no one manages the legal, communication, and executive dimensions — which is why serious incidents need someone owning the whole picture, a concept developed fully in the advanced incident-command course."],
ex:"During a breach, the pre-assigned incident commander coordinates: directing the technical team's containment while ensuring legal assesses notification obligations, communications prepares messaging, and executives are briefed for the decisions requiring their authority. Because roles were assigned in preparation, each function proceeds in parallel rather than the technical team being pulled in every direction. Clear roles turned a potentially chaotic response into a coordinated one.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2, incident response team models","Roles, team structures, and coordination."],["FEMA/ICS incident command concepts","The incident commander model adapted for cyber."]],
kc:[
{q:"The incident commander role primarily:",o:["Does all the technical work","Coordinates the overall response, sets priorities, and ensures every function is covered","Writes the malware analysis","Replaces the SOC"],a:1,x:"The commander owns the whole picture and coordination, freeing technical responders to focus on investigation and containment."},
{q:"Serious incident responses often falter when:",o:["Too many analysts help","The technical team contains the threat but no one manages the legal, communication, and executive dimensions","The commander is present","Roles are pre-assigned"],a:1,x:"Non-technical functions (legal, comms, executive decisions) are essential; without owned coordination they get dropped."},
{q:"Pre-assigning IR roles during preparation results in:",o:["Slower response","A response that flows, with functions proceeding in parallel rather than improvised at every decision point","More confusion","No benefit"],a:1,x:"Defined roles let each function proceed without improvisation, which is why role assignment is part of preparation."}]},
{t:"Malware and Ransomware Response Basics",d:"Handling the most common incident types.",
obj:["Describe the response priorities for a malware infection","Explain ransomware-specific response considerations","Explain the do-not-pay-first and containment priorities"],
read:["Malware and ransomware are the most common serious incidents, and they have specific response patterns. For general malware, the priorities are: isolate the infected host to stop spread and C2 (module eight), determine what the malware is and does (is it a commodity infostealer or an APT foothold? — the actor-hypothesis logic from BC-101), assess what it accessed or exfiltrated, then eradicate and recover. The critical early judgment is scope: a single infection may be isolated, or it may be the visible tip of a wider intrusion, which analysis must determine before declaring it contained.",
"Ransomware adds urgency and specific considerations. Because it spreads fast and destroys availability, containment is immediate and aggressive — isolating affected systems and, if spreading rapidly, segments or the whole network. Recovery depends entirely on the backup discipline from BC-101: organizations with tested, immutable, offline backups recover without paying; those without face a terrible choice. The response prioritizes stopping the spread, preserving evidence (ransomware incidents often involve prior data theft — double extortion), and restoring from clean backups, with the pay-or-not decision a business and legal matter handled by leadership, not a technical default. The preparation lesson is stark: the organizations that weather ransomware are the ones that built recoverable backups before the attack."],
ex:"A ransomware outbreak begins spreading across file servers. The team immediately isolates the affected segment to halt spread, confirms the scope, preserves evidence (finding indicators of prior data exfiltration — double extortion), and begins recovery from immutable offline backups, restoring operations without paying. The pre-built backup capability, not any heroics during the incident, determined that the organization recovered rather than paid — preparation was decisive.",
refs:[["CISA #StopRansomware Guide","Ransomware response and recovery priorities."],["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2 and SP 800-83","Malware incident handling."]],
kc:[
{q:"The critical early judgment for a malware infection is:",o:["The malware's file size","Scope — whether it is an isolated infection or the visible tip of a wider intrusion","The antivirus vendor","The time of day"],a:1,x:"A single infection may be contained or may signal a broader compromise; analysis must determine scope before declaring containment."},
{q:"An organization's ability to recover from ransomware without paying depends primarily on:",o:["Negotiation skill","Tested, immutable, offline backups built before the attack","The ransom amount","The malware type"],a:1,x:"Recoverable backups (the BC-101 discipline) determine whether recovery is possible without paying — preparation is decisive."},
{q:"Modern ransomware incidents often involve 'double extortion,' meaning:",o:["Two separate attacks","Data was stolen before encryption, so the attacker also threatens to leak it","The ransom doubles","Two ransomware strains"],a:1,x:"Attackers exfiltrate data before encrypting, adding leak threats — so response must assess and preserve exfiltration evidence."}]},
{t:"Post-Incident Activity and IR Capstone",d:"Learning, improving, and running the full lifecycle.",
obj:["Explain the lessons-learned process and its value","Describe feeding improvements back into preparation","Run the incident response lifecycle end to end"],
read:["The lifecycle closes with post-incident activity: the lessons-learned review, held after recovery, that asks what happened, how the response went, what worked, what did not, and what should change. Conducted blamelessly (like the reporting culture of BC-109), it turns the incident into improvement — updating the plan, closing the gaps the incident exposed, adding detections for the techniques used, and strengthening the controls that failed. This is the feedback loop that makes the lifecycle cyclical: each incident should make the organization measurably better prepared for the next, or the same failures recur.",
"The capstone runs the full lifecycle on one incident. Detection and analysis confirm and scope a ransomware intrusion; classification rates it critical; containment isolates hosts, blocks C2, and disables compromised credentials across host, network, and identity layers; eradication rebuilds from clean images and removes persistence; recovery restores from known-good state under heightened monitoring; evidence is preserved with chain of custody throughout; documentation builds the timeline; escalation and reporting meet internal and regulatory obligations; and the lessons-learned review feeds improvements back into preparation. Each phase depends on the preparation that preceded the incident and on the disciplines this course built.",
"The lesson the capstone drives home is that response quality is determined mostly before the incident (preparation) and measured by the whole lifecycle, not the dramatic containment moment alone. The graded deliverable — running a triage workflow, drafting an escalation notice, and completing an intake ticket for a mock alert — exercises the front end, but the full competency is the disciplined, documented, well-communicated progression from detection to lessons learned. An organization that runs this lifecycle well absorbs the incidents that prevention cannot stop — the resilience this entire academy is built to produce.",],
ex:"After a ransomware incident, the blameless lessons-learned review finds that the initial detection was slow (a monitoring gap) and containment was delayed by an unclear authority — both fixed in the updated plan, with a new detection added for the attacker's initial-access technique. When a similar attack comes months later, it is detected and contained far faster. The review turned one incident into durable improvement — the lifecycle's feedback loop doing exactly its job.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2, Post-Incident Activity","The lessons-learned process and improvement loop."],["SANS, incident response lifecycle and lessons learned","Running and improving the full process."]],
kc:[
{q:"The lessons-learned review should be conducted:",o:["To assign blame","Blamelessly, to turn the incident into improvement — updating plans, closing gaps, adding detections","Only if the incident was large","Never"],a:1,x:"A blameless review (like the reporting culture) feeds improvements back into preparation, making the lifecycle cyclical."},
{q:"Incident response quality is determined mostly:",o:["During the dramatic containment moment","Before the incident, by preparation — and measured by the whole lifecycle, not one phase","By luck","After recovery only"],a:1,x:"Preparation shapes how well every phase goes; the competency is the full disciplined lifecycle, not the containment moment alone."},
{q:"The feedback loop from post-incident activity into preparation ensures:",o:["Incidents never recur","Each incident makes the organization measurably better prepared — or the same failures repeat","Nothing changes","Blame is assigned"],a:1,x:"Feeding lessons back into preparation is what makes the lifecycle cyclical and drives continuous improvement in resilience."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: An organization builds its IR plan and access controls during an active ransomware event. This illustrates that:",o:["Improvisation is best","Preparation is the foundation — building capability during an incident means already losing","Plans are unnecessary","Speed does not matter"],a:1,x:"The tools, plans, and authorities assembled beforehand determine the response; building them mid-incident is a losing position."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An analyst dismisses an alert about a beaconing connection from a vacationing user's workstation as noise. This is:",o:["Good triage","Under-triaging — context (beaconing, absent user, new IP) marked it a real incident that was missed","Over-triaging","Correct"],a:1,x:"Dismissing a context-rich genuine alert is exactly how breaches are missed despite detection firing."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Responders isolate one host based on the first alert, but the attacker was on ten. The failure was:",o:["Too much analysis","Acting on containment before adequate scoping analysis, leaving nine compromised hosts","Isolating at all","Too fast recovery"],a:1,x:"Incomplete analysis yields partial containment; the attacker continues from the hosts the rushed response missed."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A timeline built from logs across systems is unreliable and self-contradictory. The most likely cause is:",o:["Too many logs","Clock skew — the systems were not time-synchronized, so events cannot be reliably sequenced","Encryption","Too few logs"],a:1,x:"Cross-source correlation demands synchronized clocks; skew corrupts the order and causation the timeline depends on."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A responder powers off a compromised host immediately. This risks:",o:["Nothing","Destroying volatile memory evidence and alerting the attacker — isolation preserves state better","Faster recovery","Better forensics"],a:1,x:"Power-off loses volatile evidence; network isolation stops the attacker while preserving the running state for forensics."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A team isolates compromised hosts but leaves stolen domain credentials valid. The attacker returns. The missed step was:",o:["Better antivirus","Identity-layer containment — disabling accounts and resetting credentials/KRBTGT","More hosts isolated","Faster reporting"],a:1,x:"Credential theft drives lateral movement; without identity-layer containment, host isolation leaves the attacker a way back."},
{q:"SCENARIO: After eradicating obvious malware, an organization is reinfected days later. The likely reason:",o:["The malware was too strong","A persistence mechanism (backdoor account, secondary implant) was missed by incomplete eradication","Recovery was too slow","The backup failed"],a:1,x:"Attackers plant multiple persistence mechanisms; missing one enables return — thorough eradication and monitoring are essential."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An insider-theft investigation's evidence is challenged in an HR proceeding. It holds up because the team:",o:["Investigated on the original file","Imaged and hashed the evidence, worked on copies, and kept a chain-of-custody log","Skipped documentation","Relied on memory"],a:1,x:"Hashing, working on copies, and an unbroken custody trail prove the evidence unaltered and authentic — preserving its value."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A confirmed personal-data breach has a 72-hour regulatory notification deadline. Preparation mapped this obligation, so the team:",o:["Researches the law mid-incident","Meets the deadline because obligations and contacts were mapped in advance","Ignores the deadline","Waits until recovery is complete"],a:1,x:"Tight breach-notification timelines require the obligations to be understood beforehand; missing them adds a compliance failure."},
{q:"SCENARIO: After an incident, a blameless lessons-learned review adds a detection and fixes an authority gap; the next similar attack is handled far faster. This demonstrates:",o:["Luck","The lifecycle's feedback loop — post-incident learning feeding preparation drives continuous improvement","That reviews are pointless","That the first response was perfect"],a:1,x:"Feeding lessons back into preparation makes the lifecycle cyclical, turning each incident into durable resilience improvement."}
]};
